# {{ title }} web

The Next.js and shadcn/ui front end of {{ title }}. The browser talks only to this server.
`@navree/next` is its backend-for-frontend: the route handlers in `app/api`, `app/admin` and
`app/broadcasting` (`lib/navree.ts`) keep the Navree JWTs in httpOnly cookies, refresh them, check the
`Origin` of every write and forward each call to the Navree API (`NAVREE_API_URL`) with the bearer
token and the `X-Workspace` the browser names. `proxy.ts` sends a browser without a session from the
admin panel to the sign-in page.

What lives here, apart from the screens:

- `lib/navree.ts`: the app's `createNavree()`.
- `lib/social.ts`: the routes only this kit has, social sign-in (`app/auth`) and the verification link
  (`app/api/auth/email`). They end like a password sign-in.
- `lib/queries.ts`: TanStack Query hooks; the ability of the API's rules comes from `@navree/client/abilities`.
- `app/(app)/w/[workspace]/layout.tsx`: live updates of a workspace (`RealtimeProvider` and `useChannel` of
  `@navree/next/realtime`).

```sh
pnpm install     # see "The Navree packages" first
pnpm dev         # or `navree dev` in the app root, which starts the API too
pnpm test
```

## The Navree packages

{% if navree_js_dir %}
`@navree/next` and `@navree/client` are not on npm yet. `package.json` links them from the Navree
checkout this app was made from (`{{ navree_js_dir }}`). Build them once, then install:

```sh
pnpm --dir {{ navree_js_dir }} install
pnpm --dir {{ navree_js_dir }} build
pnpm install
```

The choice, and what it needs:

- The packages ship a built `dist/`, so build them again when their source changes.
- A link is a symlink, so it never goes stale and pnpm needs nothing from the registry for it. A copy
  of the packages (`file:`) could not install, because they depend on each other with `workspace:*`,
  which only resolves inside `packages/js`.
- A linked package sits outside this directory, and its own `node_modules` holds its own `react`,
  `react-dom` and `next`. So `next.config.ts` sets Next's root to the directory that holds both this
  app and the checkout (`turbopack.root`, `outputFileTracingRoot`) and compiles the packages with the
  app (`transpilePackages`). Next resolves `react` and `react-dom` for the whole bundle to its own
  copy, so the linked components run on one React.
- `pnpm-lock.yaml` pins every other package. It does not list the two links, so the first
  `pnpm install` adds them: run it without `--frozen-lockfile` and commit the result.
- If you move this app or the checkout, change the path in `package.json` and `next.config.ts`.

When the packages are published, replace the two `link:` paths with version ranges
(`"@navree/next": "{{ navree_js_range }}"`) and delete the `turbopack`, `outputFileTracingRoot` and
`transpilePackages` settings in `next.config.ts`.
{% else %}
`package.json` asks npm for `@navree/next` and `@navree/client` at `{{ navree_js_range }}`. They are
not on npm yet, so until they are published, install them from a Navree checkout: build them
(`pnpm --dir <checkout>/packages/js install`, then `build`) and replace the two ranges in
`package.json` with `link:<checkout>/packages/js/next` and `link:<checkout>/packages/js/client`.

A linked package sits outside this directory, so `next.config.ts` then needs Next's root to be the
directory that holds both this app and the checkout, and the packages compiled with the app:

```ts
const nextConfig: NextConfig = {
  turbopack: { root: "/path/that/holds/both" },
  outputFileTracingRoot: "/path/that/holds/both",
  transpilePackages: ["@navree/next", "@navree/client", "@navree/bff"],
};
```

`pnpm-lock.yaml` pins every other package. It does not list the two `@navree` packages, so the first
`pnpm install` adds them: run it without `--frozen-lockfile` and commit the result.
{% endif %}
